## VERDICT: PASS

Implementing this plan as written produces a green suite and closes GH #437. Three amendments were applied before this verdict rather than carried into implementation, so nothing is approved with warnings.

---

## Findings, all resolved before verdict

### P-1 (`test-failure`): a declared test would have been vacuous

Iteration 1 described test 3 as driving `stall_walk.count_session_signature_totals` "over a gate directory holding one audit record per schema category". That is not what the function reads. It reads `audit_history.read(session_id)`, a single JSONL at `<gate_dir>/<session>/audit_history.jsonl`.

An implementer following the literal wording would write per-category `.json` files into the gate directory, get an empty history back, and see the test pass **before** the change while asserting nothing. A green vacuous test guarding the phase's only end-to-end claim.

Resolved: the plan now states the seeding path through `audit_history.append`, cites the JSONL, and names the existing isolation fixture that monkeypatches both `validate_gate_artifact.GATES_DIR` and `qor.workdir.gate_dir`. Patching one and not the other leaves the second resolving to the live tree, which is the regression class `tests/test_gate_dir_hygiene.py` exists for, and the resulting session id is not matched by the conftest sweep.

### P-2 (`specification-drift`): a grep quoted narrower than its own output

LD-2 cited `grep -nE 'compute_record' qor/scripts/stall_walk.py` and reproduced line 57 only. The command returns 57 and 97. Under this repository's grep-verify discipline a cited command's output must be the observed output.

The omission understated the finding rather than overstating it: the raise reaches both escalator entry points, not one, and line 97 is precisely the path test 3 drives. Resolved by quoting both.

### P-3 (`specification-drift`): "one test" was one file

LD-3 said one test references both surfaces. It is one file containing two separate tests -- one imports the exception, another asserts a single value's schema membership. No single test touches both. The conclusion drawn was correct either way. Resolved.

### P-4 (Judge, pre-review): the third copy was implied absent

The boundaries limitation read as though only two copies of this vocabulary exist. A third does, and it is already stale: the audit-pass-to-category mapping table, 12 rows against a 15-value enum, missing `feature-test-undeclared` and `live-progress-fake`. It is the surface whose staleness vetoed Phase 261 at entry #735. Resolved by naming it, measuring it, and stating why it is deliberately left unbound here.

---

## What the reviewer re-derived and confirmed

The drift is exact and one-directional: 15 schema values, 14 frozenset values, `feature-test-undeclared` missing, and no reverse drift -- every frozenset member is in the schema, which makes set-parity and per-value acceptance equivalent here and both honest. Reachability holds: the audit skill directs emission of that value at two places, neither quotable verbatim because both carry inline code formatting, exactly as the plan states.

All three declared tests are genuinely red beforehand, each for its own stated reason, and each fails on a silently broken unit.

Blast radius is clean. `_VALID_CATEGORIES` is read only inside its own module, at the definition and one comprehension. No test asserts its size or contents. `tests/test_findings_signature.py` uses an invented value for its negative, still outside the enum after the change. `remediate_pattern_match.PATTERN_RULES` carries no category literals. The schema is unmodified, so every schema-derived test is untouched. A fourth family of generated mirrors exists under `qor/dist/variants/` and a stale copy under `build/`; neither is a consumer, and `testpaths` excludes `build/`.

